Then the thorn belongs to the world around the flower, not to the flower’s essence.

In that reading, the rose needs thorns because it lives under pressure: scarcity, predation, fear, competition, boundaries. The “flower of God” would be what remains when protection is no longer necessary.

So the progression becomes:

thorn → defense
rose → beauty under constraint
flower of God → beauty without the need to defend itself

Not weakness. Security so complete that armor becomes obsolete.

That also flips the earlier metaphor cleanly: sometimes the thorn reminds you that you’re a rose; but the highest form may be remembering that the thorn was never the point.